  planner_response {
    thinking: "**Rethinking the Architecture**\n\nI\'m now considering the best architectural approach, and I\'ve zeroed in on the specifics for `realtime_guidelines`. I\'m going to utilize WebSockets with server-side state, and run an Express server along with a WebSocket server, all on port 3000. On the front end, I\'ll be implementing React/Vite to manage the WebSocket connection. I\'m confirming that the user wants to utilize Gemini for translating the speech.\n\n\n**Refining the Workflow**\n\nI\'ve revised the architecture and realized I don\'t need Gemini\'s Live API; a standard translation setup is sufficient. The revised approach uses `window.SpeechRecognition` on Client 1 for transcription and a WebSocket connection to the server. The server then translates the text via Gemini and broadcasts the original and translated text to Client 2. Client 2 receives the texts, displays them, and uses `window.speechSynthesis` to speak the translation. It\'s a bit more browser-dependent, but simpler overall.\n\n\n**Investigating Gemini API**\n\nI\'m now diving into the Gemini Live Translation API to see if it fits. The `gemini-3.5-live-translate-preview` model appears to translate audio to audio and text transcripts. However, it looks like it\'s limited to one target language per session, which complicates things since there are two people involved. The \"live transcript\" requirement presents another challenge. I\'m re-evaluating the possibility of `window.SpeechRecognition`.\n\n\n**Simplifying the Implementation**\n\nI\'ve decided to streamline things significantly. I\'m opting for a text-based approach: Client A uses `SpeechRecognition` to transcribe speech, and sends the transcription and language data to the server via WebSocket. The server then uses Gemini to translate and broadcast the original and translated text to all clients. Each client displays the results, and client B uses `speechSynthesis` to speak the translated text. This is much simpler, more robust, and fully satisfies the requirements, and should be relatively easy to get working in a mobile-first web app.\n\n\n**Prioritizing a Manual Entry**\n\nI\'ve decided to prioritize a manual code entry field for simplicity and to avoid potential camera permission issues within the iframe. I\'ll stick with that approach to keep things clean and efficient. Next, I\'ll be setting up the Express + Vite server as detailed, and then implementing the server-side logic with Socket.IO and the Gemini API, along with the UI components on the front end.\n\n\n**Defining the Dependencies**\n\nI\'m now focusing on setting up the dependencies. I will add the microphone to the `metadata.json` since we need it for voice input. I plan to use manual code entry as the primary input method and may include a QR scanner later, but it will only be for the QR display/generation. Currently, I\'m installing all the necessary packages. I am setting up the development environment, updating `package.json`, and adjusting `metadata.json` for permissions. After that, I\'ll work on the backend server with Express, Socket.IO, rooms based on session codes, and events to handle communication and translation with Gemini. Finally, the plan is to build the frontend with routing and UI components.\n\n\n"
    thinking_duration {
      seconds: 12
      nanos: 417774454
    }
    stop_reason: STOP_REASON_STOP_PATTERN
