  planner_response {
    thinking: "**Investigating TTS Logic**\n\nI\'m currently focused on the TTS logic within `src/components/CallRoom.tsx`. I\'m starting by carefully analyzing how the translation service triggers the TTS, looking for potential areas where the trigger might be failing or being incorrectly handled on the receiving device. I\'m prioritizing the call and the related devices, to determine their impact on the system.\n\n\n**Analyzing Language Handling**\n\nI\'ve been examining the `speakText` function within `CallRoom.tsx`. My focus is on the `langCode` passed to it, specifically `msg.targetLang`. I\'m tracing where this `targetLang` originates. I\'m checking `server.ts` to see what information is sent to the client as `msg.targetLang`. I\'m starting to get to the root of the problem.\n\n\n**Clarifying Language Codes**\n\nI\'ve discovered a critical discrepancy. It seems the `targetLang` being sent to the client is the language name (\"English\"), not the required BCP-47 code (\"en-US\"). The server receives the language name from `App.tsx` and `JoinCall.tsx`. The `SpeechSynthesisUtterance.lang` property strictly needs a BCP-47 tag. This mismatch is a likely cause of the TTS failure, as the browser won\'t know how to handle \"English\". Moreover, a browser restriction of user interaction before `speechSynthesis` can start might be blocking the sounds.\n\n\n**Refining BCP-47 Implementation**\n\nI\'m now zeroing in on the best method to transmit `bcp47` language codes throughout the system. A key realization is that the client already has the language object, including the `bcp47` code, on the receiving end. The challenge is in connecting the right code to `utterance.lang`. I\'m going to look at the client-side code, particularly how it receives the language data, and how the `speakText` function could use that information, instead of relying solely on the server to send the BCP-47 code.\n\n\n**Optimizing TTS Trigger**\n\nI now understand that the client already knows its own language! Therefore, inside `CallRoom.tsx`, I can directly use `language.bcp47` within the `speakText` function, making it much simpler. Specifically, `speakText(msg.translatedText, language.bcp47);` should suffice, as the receiver\'s language code is available. I\'m also now testing a few things: checking the speaker ID comparison, as well as the need to load voices using `window.speechSynthesis.getVoices()`. Furthermore, I am considering the impact of iOS/Safari autoplay restrictions on `window.speechSynthesis`.\n\n\n**Implementing Silent Startup**\n\nI\'m now implementing a silent speech synthesis play on initial user interaction to address potential browser autoplay restrictions. I am considering adding the silent synthesis within `CallRoom.tsx` in a way that handles the case where voices haven\'t loaded yet. I need to make sure I am checking for voice availability, particularly on different browsers.\n\n\n**Confirming Target Language**\n\nI\'m verifying that changing `speakText` to accept `language.bcp47` is working as intended. The fix should be: `speakText(msg.translatedText, language.bcp47);`. I\'m verifying the fix within the `useEffect` handling of `new_message` within `CallRoom.tsx`. I am also checking the error event handling.\n\n\n**Updating useEffect Dependencies**\n\nI\'ve realized a critical detail regarding the `useEffect` hook within `CallRoom.tsx`. The dependency array currently only includes `socket` and `myId`. I need to ensure that the `language` dependency is included, specifically using `language.bcp47`. This prevents potential stale closures and ensures the correct BCP-47 language code is always available when handling new messages. This is a crucial update to ensure the translated text speaks with the correct voice.\n\n\n**Initializing Speech Synthesis**\n\nI\'m now focusing on initializing speech synthesis to bypass potential browser restrictions. The optimal moment to \"warm up\" `speechSynthesis` seems to be during button clicks in `Home.tsx` and `JoinCall.tsx`, ideally when the user joins or starts the call. I\'ve formulated a `warmUpSpeechSynthesis` utility function that creates and speaks a silent utterance to trigger the engine. I\'m testing its implementation within `App.tsx`, because that code handles screen changes from button clicks. My intention is to ensure this silent initialization happens when the user takes an action, but before they receive messages.\n\n\n\n**Verifying Server-Client Logic**\n\nI\'m now checking the server-side code in `server.ts` to ensure that language data is correctly handled and transmitted to the client upon joining a room. I\'m focusing specifically on the data structure used to store and share language information between clients within a room, looking for any potential discrepancies or errors in the process. I am specifically checking that the target language is what it is supposed to be.\n\n\n**Refining Room Language Logic**\n\nI\'m now carefully examining the `server.ts` code, especially the logic handling room languages. It seems `targetLang` is correctly assigned the language name of the other user in the room, perfect for the LLM translation prompt. The issue arises later, when the client receives the language name instead of the BCP-47 code. I\'m focusing on ensuring the correct code is transmitted, instead of the name.\n\n\n"
    thinking_duration {
